WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:05.190
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:12.400
Today we are going take a collection of photos
like these, and magically, create a video

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.910
where we can fly through these photos.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.760
So, how is this even possible?

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:23.740
Especially that the input is only a handful
of photos.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:30.970
Well, typically, we give it to a learning
algorithm and ask it to synthesize a photorealistic

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:35.059
video where we fly through the scene as we
please.

00:00:35.059 --> 00:00:37.879
Of course, that sounds impossible.

00:00:37.879 --> 00:00:45.390
Especially that some information is given
about the scene, but this is really not much.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:51.020
And as you see, this is not impossible at
all - through the power of learning-based

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:58.410
techniques, this previous AI is already capable
of pulling off this amazing trick.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:05.750
And today, I am going to show you that through
this incredible new paper, with a little expertise,

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:11.420
something like this can be done even at home,
on our own machines.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:12.470
Why?

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:18.730
Because now, research scientists at Google
and Harvard also published their take on this

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:19.760
problem.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:26.370
And they promise two fantastic improvements:
Improvement number one.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:27.670
Unbounded scenes.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.220
No more front-facing scene with a stationary
camera.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:37.100
They say that now, we can rotate the camera
around the object, and their technique will

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.380
still work.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.210
That is a huge deal…if it indeed works.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.040
We will see.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:48.960
You know what, hold on to your papers, and,
let’s see together right now.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:49.960
Wow!

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:55.900
This does not look like an AI-made video out
of a bunch of photos.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.580
This looks like reality!

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.400
My goodness.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:06.050
And, of course, you are experienced Fellow
Scholars over there, so I bet you are immediately

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:12.159
interested in the fidelity of the geometry,
which truly is a sight to behold.

00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:18.769
I am a light transport researcher by trade,
so I am additionally looking at the specular

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:20.049
highlights.

00:02:20.049 --> 00:02:23.480
This is as close to reality as it gets.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:29.049
The depth maps it produces, which describe
the distance of these objects from the camera,

00:02:29.049 --> 00:02:32.969
and, they are also silky smooth.

00:02:32.969 --> 00:02:34.069
Outstanding.

00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:41.290
So, is this a one-off result, or is this a
robust technique that works on a variety of

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:42.290
scenes?

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.240
Well, I bet you know the answer by now.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.469
But wait, we talked about two promises.

00:02:48.469 --> 00:02:54.689
Promise number one was the unbounded scenes
with the moving camera.

00:02:54.689 --> 00:02:57.180
What is promise number two?

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.579
Well, promise number two is free antialiasing.

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:02.680
Ooh boy!

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:08.900
This is a technique from computer graphics
that helps us overcome these jagged edges

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.359
that are usually present in lower resolution
images.

00:03:12.359 --> 00:03:18.420
And, it really works so well, check out this
comparison against a previous work by the

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.189
name mip-NERF.

00:03:21.189 --> 00:03:27.859
And it is just so much better, the new method
truly seems to be in a league of its own.

00:03:27.859 --> 00:03:28.990
We get smoother lines.

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:36.439
And pretty much every material, and every
piece of the geometry comes out better.

00:03:36.439 --> 00:03:40.709
And note that this previous method is not
some ancient technique.

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:41.930
No-no!

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:45.560
mip-NERF is a technique from just a year ago.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.120
Bravo Google and Bravo Harvard!

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.010
And remember, all this comes from just a quick
camera scan.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:58.620
And the rest of the details is learned and
filled in by an AI.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:04.829
And this technique now truly has a remarkable
understanding of the world around us.

00:04:04.829 --> 00:04:11.620
So, just using a commodity camera, walking
around a scene, and creating a digital video

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.200
game version of it?

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.260
Absolutely incredible.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:17.030
Sign me up, right now!

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:19.329
So, what do you think?

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:21.769
What else could this be useful for?

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:25.210
What do you expect to happen a couple more
papers down the line?

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.310
Please let me know in the comments below.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:46.069
I’d love to hear 

00:04:46.069 --> 00:04:55.729
your thoughts.

00:04:55.729 --> 00:05:26.091
Thanks for watching and 
for your generous support, and I'll see you

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:28.309
next time!

